WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:10.768
In a game, look at a wood floor or a brick wall far away. If the texture is not filtered, it sparkles. Patterns crawl and shimmer every time the camera moves.

2
00:00:11.118 --> 00:00:16.089
Turn on mipmaps, and the far floor calms down. It goes a little soft instead.

3
00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:23.969
Most people say mipmaps blur things because they are far away. That is not really the reason. So what is going on?

4
00:00:25.169 --> 00:00:30.951
Here is the same room, from the same camera. On the left, no mipmap. On the right, a mipmap.

5
00:00:31.301 --> 00:00:40.261
Without it, the wood grain sparkles, and the small letters on the wall turn into a moire pattern, false wavy lines that are not in the texture.

6
00:00:40.611 --> 00:00:44.109
With it, the wood and letters go soft. Soft, but honest.

7
00:00:44.459 --> 00:00:52.203
Down a hallway it is the same story. The far tiles crawl on the left. The near doors look identical on both sides.

8
00:00:53.403 --> 00:01:01.531
The key number is the footprint, written rho. It is how many texture pixels, called texels, land inside one screen pixel.

9
00:01:01.881 --> 00:01:12.591
Up close, one texel covers several pixels. Far away, many texels crowd into one pixel. At rho equals eight, eight texels across share a single pixel.

10
00:01:12.941 --> 00:01:19.021
But each pixel only takes one sample. It is like trying to describe eight stripes with a single dot.

11
00:01:20.221 --> 00:01:29.074
There is a hard rule for sampling. A grid of pixels can only show patterns up to half a cycle per pixel. That limit is called Nyquist.

12
00:01:29.424 --> 00:01:37.168
Anything finer does not just disappear. It folds back, and shows up as a slower, fake pattern. That is called aliasing.

13
00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:48.526
In words, a texture frequency folds whenever it is above one over two times rho. At rho of eight, anything finer than one cycle per sixteen texels folds.

14
00:01:48.876 --> 00:01:58.967
And that is the aha. The sparkle is not noise. It is real fine detail, folded into fake patterns, because the pixel grid is too coarse to hold it.

15
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:04.924
To see it cleanly, use a test pattern of rings that get finer toward the edge.

16
00:02:05.274 --> 00:02:10.799
Shrink it to rho eight with plain sampling, and fake rings appear everywhere. That is pure moire.

17
00:02:11.149 --> 00:02:15.693
Smooth sampling between neighbours softens it, but the fake rings are still there.

18
00:02:16.043 --> 00:02:23.083
Now use a mipmap. The fake rings are gone. What remains is the part of the pattern a pixel can legally show.

19
00:02:24.283 --> 00:02:33.350
So a mipmap is not a distance blur. It is a legal limit. It removes exactly the detail that the pixel grid cannot hold, before sampling.

20
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:39.972
Each level is made by averaging two by two blocks of the level above. That averaging is the filter.

21
00:02:40.322 --> 00:02:49.837
If you skip the averaging, and just pick every other texel, you get a smaller picture with the same fake rings. A pyramid without a filter is not a mipmap.

22
00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:56.413
We can measure it. Count the energy in the fake patterns, in a measure called A C power.

23
00:02:56.763 --> 00:03:06.704
With no mipmap, it is eight hundred ninety eight. Smooth sampling, three hundred seventy nine. With the box filtered mipmap, thirty. About thirty times less.

24
00:03:07.054 --> 00:03:12.601
The pyramid without the filter scores eight hundred ninety nine, as bad as no mipmap at all.

25
00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:19.838
One control. Up close, when one texel covers a pixel or more, mipmaps must change nothing.

26
00:03:20.188 --> 00:03:26.097
Measured difference at rho one, zero. The near doors in the hallway agree for the same reason.

27
00:03:26.447 --> 00:03:34.554
As rho climbs from one to sixteen, the folding takes over from rho four onward, and only the mipmapped row stays clean.

28
00:03:35.754 --> 00:03:44.629
What this does not prove. This was a software renderer, so no claims about how real graphics cards choose levels, or how fast they are.

29
00:03:44.979 --> 00:03:49.651
The room photos only show the effect. The numbers come from the test pattern.

30
00:03:50.001 --> 00:04:01.307
And the softness that remains on far floors is real. A round filter blurs the narrow side of a slanted footprint too much. That is the job of anisotropic filtering.

31
00:04:02.507 --> 00:04:11.595
The takeaway. A far texture packs more detail than a pixel can hold. Without a filter, that detail folds into sparkle and crawl.

32
00:04:11.945 --> 00:04:18.644
A mipmap removes it first, by averaging. The blur you see is the legal limit, not a distance effect.

33
00:04:18.994 --> 00:04:22.429
That is why mipmaps are standard in real time graphics.

34
00:04:22.779 --> 00:04:28.859
For further reading, see Williams on pyramidal parametrics, and Shannon and Nyquist on sampling.
